        "translate": [
          455,
          285
        ]
      },
      "caption": {
        "text": "Why does Greenland look enormous beside Africa on Mercator maps? The projection magnifies high latitudes, not actual land.",
        "x": 64,
        "y": 593.625,
        "width": 1152,
        "height": 102.375,
        "right": 1216,
        "bottom": 696
      },
      "font": "\"Segoe UI\", Aptos, Calibri, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, sans-serif"
    },
    {
      "id": "area-bars",
      "segmentIndex": 1,
      "progress": 0.5,
      "theme": "light",
      "motion": null,
      "caption": {
        "text": "Greenland covers about two point one seven million square kilometers. Africa covers thirty point four million: roughly fourteen times as much area.",
        "x": 64,
        "y": 593.625,
        "width": 1152,
        "height": 102.375,
        "right": 1216,
        "bottom": 696
      },
      "font": "\"Segoe UI\", Aptos, Calibri, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, sans-serif"
    },
    {
      "id": "meridians-globe",
      "segmentIndex": 2,
      "progress": 0.5,
      "theme": "light",
      "motion": null,
      "caption": {
        "text": "On a globe, meridians converge toward the poles. Distances between neighboring lines of longitude get smaller.",
        "x": 64,
        "y": 593.625,
        "width": 1152,
        "height": 102.375,
        "right": 1216,
        "bottom": 696
      },
      "font": "\"Segoe UI\", Aptos, Calibri, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, sans-serif"
    },
    {
      "id": "meridians-map",
      "segmentIndex": 3,
      "progress": 0.5,
      "theme": "light",
      "motion": null,
      "caption": {
        "text": "Mercator draws meridians as equally spaced parallel lines. This stretches east-west distances more at high latitudes.",
        "x": 64,
        "y": 593.625,
        "width": 1152,
        "height": 102.375,
        "right": 1216,
        "bottom": 696
      },
      "font": "\"Segoe UI\", Aptos, Calibri, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, sans-serif"
    },
    {
      "id": "latitude-stretch",
      "segmentIndex": 4,
      "progress": 0.5,
      "theme": "light",
      "motion": null,
      "caption": {
        "text": "To preserve local angles, north-south distances must stretch equally. That is why latitude lines spread apart.",
        "x": 64,
        "y": 593.625,
        "width": 1152,
        "height": 102.375,
        "right": 1216,
        "bottom": 696
      },
      "font": "\"Segoe UI\", Aptos, Calibri, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, sans-serif"
    },
    {
      "id": "local-patch",
      "segmentIndex": 5,
      "progress": 0.5,
      "theme": "light",
      "motion": null,
      "caption": {
        "text": "Consider a tiny patch at sixty degrees latitude, in a spherical model with scale one at the equator.",
        "x": 64,
        "y": 593.625,
        "width": 1152,
        "height": 102.375,
        "right": 1216,
        "bottom": 696
      },
      "font": "\"Segoe UI\", Aptos, Calibri, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, sans-serif"
    },
    {
      "id": "local-area",
      "segmentIndex": 6,
      "progress": 0.5,
      "theme": "light",
      "motion": null,
      "caption": {
        "text": "Locally, width doubles and height doubles: four times the area. This is not one factor for all Greenland.",
        "x": 64,
        "y": 593.625,
        "width": 1152,
        "height": 102.375,
        "right": 1216,
        "bottom": 696
      },
      "font": "\"Segoe UI\", Aptos, Calibri, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, sans-serif"
    },
